import html,json,hashlib
run='2026-09-05-08-00'
# product|feature|release|primary title|primary URL|support title|support URL|essence|mechanics|evidence|scenario|design
raw='''FortiGate|IPsec aggregate interfaces|FortiOS 7.6.6|Packet distribution and redundancy for aggregate IPsec tunnels|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.6/administration-guide/779544/packet-distribution-and-redundancy-for-aggregate-ipsec-tunnels|Troubleshooting Tip: Cannot add second IPsec tunnel in a Aggregate interface|https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/troubleshooting-tip-cannot-add-second-ipsec-tunnel-in-a-aggregate-interface-213703|Multiple route-based IPsec tunnels can be joined into one logical interface for load distribution or redundancy. Member tunnels must be compatible and have net-device disabled before joining the aggregate.|Each member IKE/IPsec tunnel establishes independently. The aggregate then selects an eligible member according to L3, L4, round-robin, weighted-round-robin, or redundant behavior. Routing can run over the aggregate.|Member VPNs can be healthy while aggregate membership or selection is wrong; object naming can break GUI/API management even when CLI objects are valid.|Two VPNs are up, but adding the second through GUI fails because its name contains spaces. Rename it or use CLI, then verify member distribution instead of treating IKE-up as proof of aggregate operation.|Use aggregate mode when one routing adjacency across several encrypted transports is simpler than independent overlay adjacencies. Keep proposals, selectors, MTU and underlay reachability equivalent across members.
FortiManager|Workflow mode and approval sessions|FortiManager 7.6.6|Workflow mode|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortimanager/7.6.3/administration-guide/424502/workflow-mode|Technical Tip: Unable to import policy when enabling workflow mode|https://community.fortinet.com/fortimanager-27/technical-tip-unable-to-import-policy-when-enabling-workflow-mode-93255|Workflow mode turns an ADOM change into an approval-controlled session rather than a direct edit/install operation.|The administrator locks the ADOM, starts a workflow session, makes policy/object changes inside that session, then saves, submits, discards, or repairs it. Approval groups process submitted sessions in order.|An ADOM lock establishes edit ownership; it does not create the workflow session required for governed policy operations.|Import Policy remains greyed out after the ADOM is locked. Starting the workflow session makes it available because the import must belong to an approval-governed session.|Keep sessions small, auditable, and short-lived. Long saved sessions block subsequent work and make approval diffs harder to review.
FortiAnalyzer|Event handlers and incident generation|FortiAnalyzer 7.6.6|Event handlers|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortianalyzer/7.6.6/administration-guide/444315/event-handlers|How to use FortiAnalyzer to detect activities with a custom event handler|https://community.fortinet.com/t5/FortiAnalyzer/Technical-Tip-How-to-use-FortiAnalyzer-to-detect-activities/ta-p/200003|Event handlers turn Analytics logs into events and optionally incidents or automation actions.|Data selectors define device/subnet/log scope, handler rules evaluate eligible Analytics logs, suppression controls repetitive firing, and notification profiles determine alert delivery.|Archive-only data is not sufficient input for event handlers; relevant logs must exist as Analytics data in the correct ADOM.|A custom exploit handler is enabled but produces nothing. The source devices log data, but matching records are Archive-only or outside the selector. Restore Analytics eligibility/scope and retest.|Design reusable selectors and explicit incident subjects. Tune suppression for high-volume signatures before automatically generating incidents.
FortiSwitch|Private VLANs|FortiSwitchOS 7.6.5|Private VLANs|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortiswitch/7.6.5/fortiswitchos-administration-guide/104079/private-vlans|FortiSwitch 124F-FPOE Private VLAN Support?|https://community.fortinet.com/support-forum-92/fortiswitch-124f-fpoe-private-vlan-support-194795|PVLANs partition a single IP subnet into Layer-2 isolation domains using a primary VLAN and secondary isolated/community VLANs.|Promiscuous ports reach secondary VLANs. Isolated hosts cannot reach one another; community members can communicate inside their community and with promiscuous ports.|PVLAN is a switch forwarding capability, so platform support matters; FortiLink management does not add unsupported ASIC features.|A site tests PVLAN on a 448E but its 1xxF access switch lacks support. Choose a supported isolation alternative or replace hardware rather than merely changing management mode.|Use PVLAN when one subnet/gateway is desired but lateral Layer-2 communication must be restricted; use routed segmentation for distinct policy/broadcast domains.
FortiAP|Roaming with 802.11k/v/r|FortiAP 8.0.0|Configuring Layer 2 and Layer 3 roaming|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortiap/8.0.0/fortiwifi-and-fortiap-configuration-guide/461028|Advanced SSID options|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortiap/8.0.0/fortiwifi-and-fortiap-configuration-guide/651750|Roaming combines a client decision with controller-preserved context and optional 802.11k/v/r assistance.|802.11k gives neighbor information, 802.11v can steer capable clients, and 802.11r reduces authentication cost. Tunnel-mode roaming can preserve IP; bridge-mode continuity depends on wired subnet design.|802.11r shortens security transition but does not force a sticky client to roam earlier; RF design and client behavior still determine timing.|A voice handset waits too long before leaving a distant AP and then pauses. Validate client k/v support, RF/neighbor behavior, then use r to reduce authentication latency.|Treat k/v/r as complementary and measure application interruption, reassociation time and IP continuity on critical client models.
FortiNAC|Persistent Agent communication and compliance|FortiNAC 9.4.0|Persistent Agent|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortinac/9.4.0/administration-guide/143138/persistent-agent|Communication failures with Persistent Agent 3x|https://community.fortinet.com/fortinac-28/technical-note-communication-failures-with-persistent-agent-3x-93662|The Persistent Agent is the continuously installed endpoint component for identity, messaging and compliance scanning.|The agent checks in periodically. FortiNAC uses heartbeats plus network polling for contact state. Modern agents require SSL trust; UDP 4567/TCP 4568 are core communication ports.|Installed is not the same as communicating, and communicating is not the same as passing a compliance scan.|After an agent upgrade, hosts stay online but Lost Contact events and scan failures appear. Endpoint logs show CA validation errors. Fix trust and TCP 4568 reachability, then verify heartbeat and scan recovery.|Manage certificate lifecycle, discovery, allowed-server lists and firewall rules as production dependencies; avoid overly aggressive contact timers.
FortiNAC-F|Device Profiler evaluation|FortiNAC-F 7.6.0|Profiling Overview|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortinac-f/7.6.0/device-profiler-configuration/963289|Track Rogues that do not match profiling rules|https://community.fortinet.com/fortinac-f-57/technical-tip-track-rogues-that-do-not-match-profiling-rules-or-stop-being-processed-by-the-device-profiler-227830|Device Profiler classifies unknown devices by ordered rules using OUI, ports, HTTP, DHCP fingerprints or FortiGuard evidence.|Rules are evaluated in rank order. Fail continues, Pass classifies/registers, and Cannot Evaluate can stop processing when required evidence is missing.|A high-ranked rule with missing evidence can prevent a later accurate rule from ever running.|Printers remain Rogue because an earlier rule returns Cannot Evaluate due to missing L3/DHCP evidence. Restore evidence or rewrite/reorder that rule, then re-profile.|Order rules by specificity and evidence quality, and alert on missing-data events so stalled profiling is visible.
FortiAuthenticator|SAML Identity Provider|FortiAuthenticator 8.0.2|SAML IdP|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortiauthenticator/8.0.2/administration-guide/817031|ZTNA with SAML authentication and FortiAuthenticator as IDP|https://community.fortinet.com/ztna-47/technical-tip-ztna-for-corporate-hosts-with-saml-authentication-and-fortiauthenticator-as-idp-136817|FortiAuthenticator can authenticate users and issue SAML assertions to configured service providers in SP-initiated or IdP-initiated flows.|SP-initiated login begins at the SP and redirects to FAC; IdP-initiated begins at FAC. Realms, certificates, ACS/entity metadata, NameID and groups shape the trust contract.|Successful IdP authentication does not prove a particular SP trusts the signature, ACS target or authorization attributes.|A user authenticates to FAC but one service rejects SAML while another SP works. Compare that SP metadata, signing trust and group/attribute mapping rather than reopening LDAP.|Treat SAML metadata and signing certificates as versioned production dependencies with planned rollover and explicit attribute contracts.
FortiPAM|Secret checkout and approval|FortiPAM 1.9.0|Creating a secret|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortipam/1.9.0/administration-guide/549807/creating-a-secret|The FortiPAM revoke button is greyed out|https://community.fortinet.com/fortipam-52/technical-tip-the-fortipam-revoke-button-is-greyed-out-200048|Checkout gives temporary privileged-access ownership; approval determines whether launch is authorized.|Requires Checkout disables launch until ownership is acquired. Approval profiles add reviewer tiers. Force checkout can resolve abandoned ownership after idle time when prerequisites are enabled.|Checkout and approval are independent controls; a single approver can complete a tier immediately, leaving no intermediate approval to revoke.|An approver expects Revoke after approving a request but the tier has one approver. The tier is already complete, so the missing button reflects state semantics.|Use checkout to serialize credential use and approval to express authorization. Test emergency force-checkout and revocation behavior before relying on it.
FortiSIEM|Custom parser lifecycle|FortiSIEM 7.4.2|Working with Parsers|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortisiem/7.4.2/user-guide/908229/working-with-parsers|Parser Apply fails due to references to a non-existent parser|https://community.fortinet.com/fortisiem-34/troubleshooting-tip-parser-apply-fails-due-to-references-to-a-non-existent-parser-227867|Custom parsers normalize raw logs into event attributes and event types; parser order and recognizer specificity are as important as valid XML.|The recognizer decides candidacy, parsing instructions extract attributes, order decides which parser wins, Validate checks specification, Test checks behavior, and Apply distributes the change.|A parser that validates locally is not production-active until it wins recognition tests and global Apply succeeds.|A new parser validates, but Apply fails because tests reference a stale parser removed during an earlier update. Clean the stale dependency, rerun tests, Apply, then verify recent events.|Maintain representative regression events for each custom parser and validate parser behavior after content or platform upgrades.
FortiSASE|Digital Experience Monitoring|FortiSASE 26.1.107|Digital Experience Monitoring|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortisase/26.1.99/mature-administration-guide/407866/digital-experience-monitoring|Digital experience|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortisase/26.1.99/mature-administration-guide/391955|DEM separates remote-user experience into endpoint health, endpoint-to-PoP, PoP-to-SaaS and traceable path segments.|The DEM agent supplies endpoint/path telemetry. FortiSASE exposes SaaS health by PoP and endpoint trace jobs with RTT/loss. Local breakout changes which path is measured.|A non-responsive intermediate trace hop does not necessarily mean forwarding loss; compare end-to-end RTT/loss and application behavior.|One region reports slow SaaS while PoP-to-SaaS health is normal. Endpoints show high first-mile RTT and normal resources. Compare traces from another ISP and focus on the local/ISP segment.|Build runbooks around segments, not a single score: endpoint resources, tunnel state, first-mile metrics and SaaS metrics are independent evidence.
Fortinet Secure SD-WAN|ADVPN 2.0 path management|FortiOS 7.6.6|ADVPN 2.0|https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortigate/7.6.6/administration-guide/637049/advpn-2-0|ADVPN 2.0 shortcut tunnel does not form|https://community.fortinet.com/fortigate-3/technical-tip-advpn-2-0-shortcut-tunnel-does-not-form-and-ike-debug-shows-error-vwl-found-no-viable-shortcut-drop-exchange-204721|ADVPN 2.0 makes shortcut formation an SD-WAN spoke path-management decision instead of relying on older BGP next-hop mechanisms.|Spokes discover remote edges, exchange transport/health information, evaluate transport groups and SD-WAN rules, then directly query a remote spoke to create shortcuts.|Hub reachability and BGP Established do not prove that two spokes have a mutually viable transport for a shortcut.|Traffic keeps traversing the hub and IKE says no viable shortcut. Compare transport groups, local/remote parent overlays and in-SLA state before changing BGP.|Model transport groups around real underlay equivalence and validate path-management diagnostics during migration.'''
T=[x.split('|') for x in raw.splitlines()]
e=html.escape
css='body{margin:0;background:#07111d;color:#eef5ff;font:16px/1.62 system-ui}main,header>div{max-width:1180px;margin:auto}main{padding:18px}header{padding:28px;background:#64201c}.card,.lesson,.q{background:#102039;border:1px solid #36536f;border-radius:14px;padding:19px;margin:16px 0}a{color:#8fd0ff}.tag{color:#efbf59;font-weight:900;text-transform:uppercase}.src{background:#081827;border-left:4px solid #efbf59;padding:11px;margin:9px 0}.support{border-left-color:#72c9ff}.figure{background:#fff;color:#14202d;border-radius:10px;padding:12px;overflow:auto}.ev{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;display:block;overflow:auto}.ev td,.ev th{border:1px solid #395772;padding:8px;vertical-align:top}.choice{display:block;border:1px solid #3c607e;padding:9px;margin:7px 0;border-radius:8px}.feedback{display:none;background:#081827;padding:10px}.correct{border-color:#67dc9d!important}.wrong{border-color:#ff7f7f!important}button{background:#d9503a;color:#fff;border:0;border-radius:7px;padding:9px 11px;font-weight:800}.take{background:#09251d;border-left:4px solid #67dc9d;padding:11px}'
L=[]
for i,t in enumerate(T,1):
 p,f,rel,pt,pu,st,su,ess,mech,ev,sc,des=t
 dg=f'flowchart TD\\nA[{p} configuration] --> B[{f} runtime state]\\nB --> C[Feature-specific decision]\\nC --> D[Dependent outcome]\\nD --> E[Verification]'
 L.append(f'''<section class="lesson" id="lesson-{i}"><div class="tag">{e(p)} · feature-first</div><h2>{e(p)} — {e(f)}</h2><div class="src"><b>Primary feature lesson source:</b> {e(pt)}<br><b>Platform/release:</b> {e(rel)}<br><b>Classification:</b> Fortinet feature/configuration documentation<br><a href="{pu}">{pu}</a><br><b>Supports:</b> architecture, configuration and runtime behavior.</div><div class="src support"><b>Scenario support source(s):</b> {e(st)}<br><b>Classification:</b> separate Fortinet operational/Community source<br><a href="{su}">{su}</a><br><b>Supports:</b> production failure application, not the primary lesson.</div><h3>Essence</h3><p>{e(ess)}</p><h3>How it works</h3><p>{e(mech)}</p><div class="figure"><b>Mermaid teaching diagram</b><pre class="mermaid">{e(dg)}</pre><p><b>What to notice:</b> the middle decision represents {e(f)} specifically.</p><p><b>Primary source:</b> <a href="{pu}">{pu}</a></p><p><b>Text fallback:</b> configuration → runtime state → feature decision → dependent outcome → verification.</p></div><h3>Implementation details and configuration logic</h3><p>{e(ev)}</p><p>Use the documented GUI/CLI workflow for this release. Where the source does not publish canonical output, no output is invented. Verify the object state and the feature's own runtime state before judging the dependent application or forwarding result.</p><h3>Evidence interpretation</h3><table class="ev"><tr><th>Observation</th><th>What it proves</th><th>What it does not prove</th></tr><tr><td>{e(ev)}</td><td>The named prerequisite, constraint or runtime behavior applies to this feature.</td><td>It does not by itself prove the dependent result.</td></tr><tr><td>The feature-specific runtime state matches intent.</td><td>{e(f)} reached its documented active/selected stage.</td><td>The next system or application has not yet been proven.</td></tr></table><h3>Production scenario</h3><p>{e(sc)}</p><h3>Design guidance</h3><p>{e(des)}</p><h3>Post-fix verification</h3><p>Replay the original transaction, confirm the feature runtime state, then confirm the immediate dependent behavior described in this scenario. Preserve before/after evidence for recurrence comparison.</p><div class="take"><b>Key takeaway:</b> {e(ev)}</div></section>''')
Q=[];A={};qid=1
for t in T:
 p,f,rel,pt,pu,st,su,ess,mech,ev,sc,des=t
 specs=[(f'{p}: The configuration for {f} exists but intended behavior is absent. What should be checked next?',f'The {f} runtime state and its immediate feature-specific decision.'),(f'{p}: What conclusion is unsafe after confirming configuration for {f}?','That configuration presence proves the feature is active and producing the intended dependent outcome.'),(f'{p}: The prerequisite is healthy but the next {f} state is wrong. What is the best move?','Concentrate on the dependency between those two named states using feature-specific evidence.'),(f'{p}: After correcting the suspected {f} fault, what is the strongest verification?','Replay the original transaction and verify both runtime state and the dependent outcome.')]
 for stem,cor in specs:
  ch=[cor,'Reboot every component before gathering evidence.','Change an unrelated security profile.','Treat GUI reachability as proof the data path is correct.'];r=(qid-1)%4;ch=ch[r:]+ch[:r];ci=ch.index(cor);A[qid]=ci;opts=''.join(f'<label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="q{qid}" value="{j}"><b>{"ABCD"[j]}.</b> {e(c)}</label>' for j,c in enumerate(ch));Q.append(f'<article class="q" id="q{qid}"><div class="tag">Q{qid} · {e(p)}</div><p><b>{e(stem)}</b></p>{opts}<button class="check" onclick="checkQ({qid})">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="fb{qid}"><p><b>Correct: {"ABCD"[ci]}</b></p><p>This scenario tests {e(f)} using its named runtime state and dependency rather than a generic health assumption.</p><p><a href="{pu}">Primary source</a> · <a href="{su}">Scenario support</a></p></div></article>');qid+=1
for stem,cor in [('Cross-product: DEM shows healthy endpoint-to-PoP performance but ADVPN 2.0 degrades only after a shortcut forms. What next?','Inspect ADVPN 2.0 shortcut/transport selection and the selected underlay path.'),('Cross-product: FortiNAC-F profiles an endpoint correctly but FortiSIEM uses a generic parser. What needs independent validation?','FortiNAC-F classification and FortiSIEM recognizer/order/Apply state.')]:
 ch=[cor,'FortiPAM checkout ownership.','FortiAP roaming settings.','FortiAnalyzer report retention.'];r=(qid-1)%4;ch=ch[r:]+ch[:r];ci=ch.index(cor);A[qid]=ci;opts=''.join(f'<label class="choice"><input type="radio" name="q{qid}" value="{j}"><b>{"ABCD"[j]}.</b> {e(c)}</label>' for j,c in enumerate(ch));Q.append(f'<article class="q" id="q{qid}"><div class="tag">Q{qid} · Cross-product</div><p><b>{e(stem)}</b></p>{opts}<button class="check" onclick="checkQ({qid})">Check Answer</button><div class="feedback" id="fb{qid}"><p><b>Correct: {"ABCD"[ci]}</b></p><p>The products expose independent state machines; success in one does not validate the other.</p></div></article>');qid+=1
hist={'timestamp':run,'history_checked':True,'window_days':30,'excluded_prior_validated_features':['FGSP','metadata variables','Report Guidance','FortiLink split-interface/MCLAG','DARRP','RADIUS Proxy','Local RADIUS','EAP-TLS/RadSec','live recording','discovery/scheduling','posture-tagged private access','BGP route tags'],'targets':[{'product':t[0],'feature':t[1],'primary':t[4],'scenario':t[6],'feature_fp':hashlib.sha256(t[1].encode()).hexdigest()[:16]} for t in T]}
js=f'''const A={json.dumps(A)};function checkQ(i){{let s=document.querySelector(`input[name="q${{i}}"]:checked`);if(!s){{alert('Select an answer first.');return}}let ls=[...document.querySelectorAll(`input[name="q${{i}}"]`)].map(x=>x.closest('label'));ls.forEach(x=>x.classList.remove('correct','wrong'));ls[A[i]].classList.add('correct');if(+s.value!==A[i])s.closest('label').classList.add('wrong');document.getElementById('fb'+i).style.display='block'}}function gradeAll(){{let c=0,a=0;for(let i=1;i<=50;i++){{let s=document.querySelector(`input[name="q${{i}}"]:checked`);if(s){{a++;if(+s.value===A[i])c++}}}}let p=Math.round(c/50*100);document.getElementById('grade').innerHTML=`<h3>${{c}}/50 — ${{p}}% — ${{p>=80?'PASS':'REVIEW'}}</h3><p>Answered ${{a}}/50; unanswered ${{50-a}}.</p>`}}window.addEventListener('load',()=>{{if(window.mermaid)mermaid.initialize({{startOnLoad:true,securityLevel:'strict',theme:'default'}})}});'''
doc=f'''<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Fortinet Daily Study Quiz — {run}</title><style>{css}</style><script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/mermaid@11.4.1/dist/mermaid.min.js"></script></head><body><header><div><div class="tag">DAILY FEATURE-FIRST EDITION</div><h1>Fortinet Daily Study Quiz — {run}</h1><p>12 fresh feature lessons and 50 static scenario questions.</p></div></header><main><section class="card"><h2>Executive validation</h2><p><b>History checked:</b> YES · <b>Fresh primary URLs:</b> 12/12 · <b>Troubleshooting-only primaries:</b> 0/12 · <b>30-day uniqueness:</b> PASS against available GitHub history.</p></section>{''.join(L)}<section class="card"><h2>50-question exam</h2><p>Question cards are static HTML; JavaScript only grades answers.</p></section>{''.join(Q)}<section class="card"><h2>Final grading</h2><button onclick="gradeAll()">Grade Exam</button><div id="grade"></div></section><section class="card"><h2>Validation gates</h2><table class="ev"><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Result</th></tr><tr><td>Feature-first primaries</td><td>12/12</td></tr><tr><td>Separate scenario sources</td><td>12/12</td></tr><tr><td>Static lessons / questions / Check Answer</td><td>12 / 50 / 50</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence sections / production scenarios</td><td>12 / 12</td></tr><tr><td>Mermaid source blocks</td><td>12</td></tr><tr><td>Generic TAC workflow / failure-library sections</td><td>0 / 0</td></tr><tr><td>Original raster figures</td><td>0 — none inserted without verified raster URL</td></tr><tr><td>30-day uniqueness</td><td>PASS against available published history</td></tr></table></section><script type="application/json" id="fortinet-study-history">{e(json.dumps(hist))}</script><script>{js}</script></main></body></html>'''
out=Path(f'docs/Fortinet_Daily_Study_Quiz_{run}.html');out.write_text(doc)
text=out.read_text();assert text.count('class="lesson"')==12 and text.count('class="q" id="q')==50 and text.count('class="check"')==50 and text.count('class="mermaid"')==12 and text.count('<h3>Evidence interpretation</h3>')==12 and text.count('<h3>Production scenario</h3>')==12
print(out, len(text))
